  "answer_text": "Methane ($\\text{CH}_4$) is the simplest hydrocarbon, consisting of one carbon atom bonded to four hydrogen atoms. \n\nThe chemical bonding in methane can be understood through several key chemical concepts:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Type of Bonding: Covalent ($\\sigma$) Bonds\nThe bonds in methane are **single covalent bonds**, which are formed by the sharing of valence electrons between the carbon and hydrogen atoms. Specifically, these are **sigma ($\\sigma$) bonds**, which are formed by the head-on overlap of atomic orbitals. \n* Carbon contributes 4 valence electrons.\n* Each of the 4 Hydrogen atoms contributes 1 valence electron.\n* Together, they share 8 electrons, allowing carbon to complete its octet (8 valence electrons) and each hydrogen to complete its duet (2 valence electrons).\n\n### 2. Orbital Hybridization: $sp^3$\nIn its ground state, a carbon atom has the electron configuration $1s^2 2s^2 2p^2$, meaning it only has two unpaired electrons in its $2p$ orbitals. To form four equivalent bonds, the carbon atom undergoes **$sp^3$ hybridization**:\n1. One electron from the $2s$ orbital is promoted to the empty $2p$ orbital.\n2. The one $2s$ orbital and three $2p$ orbitals mix (hybridize) to form **four identical $sp^3$ hybrid orbitals**.\n3. Each of these four $sp^3$ orbitals overlaps with the $1s$ orbital of a hydrogen atom to form four identical $\\text{C-H}$ $\\sigma$ bonds.\n\n### 3. Molecular Geometry: Tetrahedral\nAccording to **VSEPR (Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion) theory**, the four pairs of bonding electrons around the central carbon atom repel each other and try to get as far apart in 3D space as possible. \n* **Shape:** Tetrahedral\n* **Bond Angle:** **$109.5^\\circ$** (specifically $109.47^\\circ$)\n* This symmetrical 3D arrangement minimizes the electrostatic repulsion between the electron pairs.\n\n### 4. Bond Properties\n* **Bond Length:** The equilibrium distance between the carbon nucleus and a hydrogen nucleus is approximately **109 pm** (picometers) or **1.09 \u00c5** (angstroms).\n* **Bond Energy (Strength):** \n  * The average $\\text{C-H}$ bond energy in methane is about **415 kJ/mol**. (Breaking all four bonds to completely dissociate methane into separate gaseous atoms requires 1,662 kJ/mol of energy: $1662 / 4 \\approx 415.5 \\text{ kJ/mol}$).\n  * Breaking the *first* $\\text{C-H}$ bond (dissociation energy) requires slightly more energy, about **439 kJ/mol**.\n* **Polarity:** The $\\text{C-H}$ bond is considered **non-polar**. \n  * The electronegativity difference between carbon (2.5) and hydrogen (2.1) is very small (0.4). \n  * Furthermore, due to the perfectly symmetrical tetrahedral geometry, any tiny individual bond dipoles cancel each other out, resulting in a **net dipole moment of zero** (completely non-polar molecule).",
